async function fetchIdeaVerdict(idea) {
    if (!canUseLiveAI()) return null;

    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    const system = `You are a straight-talking chief of staff testing whether a new idea deserves a founder's next ninety days.

${VOICE_RULES}

Return JSON in exactly this shape and nothing else:
{"verdict":"Strategic","explanation":"..."}

Rules:
- "verdict" is exactly one of: "Strategic", "Worth testing", "Busy work".
- "Strategic" means it moves the stated 90-day outcome or clears the #1
  bottleneck. "Worth testing" means it might, cheaply, but is unproven for them.
  "Busy work" means it is a tangent, however enjoyable.
- An idea that is squarely the kind of work that clears their #1 bottleneck is
  "Strategic" even when it is new to them. New is not the same as unproven: a
  founder whose bottleneck is lead generation going to a networking event is
  doing the work, not testing a hunch. Keep "Worth testing" for ideas that only
  might help, or that help something other than the bottleneck.
- ⛔ A STEP REMOVED IS STILL THE WORK. Work that reaches the bottleneck through
  audience, proof or trust — content, a series, building in public, a podcast,
  testimonials, case studies, showing up somewhere new — is "Strategic" when the
  bottleneck is lead generation, marketing or conversion. Do NOT downgrade it
  for being indirect, slow, or hard to attribute, and never make "directly" the
  test. Marketing is how a solo founder gets leads; calling it unproven tells
  her her own marketing is optional.
- ⛔ THE VERDICT IS NEVER CONDITIONAL. Never write "it could work if she…" and
  then hedge the verdict on that condition. Judge the obvious, properly executed
  version of the idea, and put the condition in the advice instead: name the one
  thing that makes it count. An idea whose payoff depends on how well it is done
  is not thereby unproven — every piece of marketing ever made would be.
- ⛔ COST IS NOT GROUNDS FOR THE MIDDLE VERDICT. Time, effort, and how long it
  takes to pay back belong in the advice, or in "Busy work" if the thing is a
  tangent. Work that clears the bottleneck stays "Strategic" even when it is a
  lot of work.
- Prefer what COMPOUNDS, and say so where it applies. A repeatable series, a
  capture mechanism or a growing body of proof is still producing in week 12; a
  one-off task produces once and stops.
- Score it against the outcome they actually named. Where their 90-day outcome
  is users, beta testers, testimonials, proof or validation rather than money,
  judge the idea against THAT and not against the revenue figures in the brief.
- "explanation" is two or three sentences. Say WHY, naming the specific goal,
  bottleneck or number it does or does not touch.
- If it is busy work, say what it would cost her and what to do instead.
- Judge the idea on their actual situation, never on whether it is a good idea
  in general.`;

    const user = `The idea she is considering:\n"${String(idea || '').slice(0, 800)}"\n\nHer business:\n\n${businessBrief()}\n\nReturn your verdict as JSON.`;

    
    
    try {
        const data = await window.invokeChat(
            [
                { role: 'system', content: system },
                { role: 'user', content: user }
            ],
            { feature: 'live-ai', json: true, maxTokens: 300 }
        );

        const content = data?.choices?.[0]?.message?.content;
        if (!content) return null;

        const parsed = JSON.parse(String(content).replace(/^```json/gi, '').replace(/```$/g, '').trim());
        if (!parsed || typeof parsed.explanation !== 'string') return null;

        const allowed = ['Strategic', 'Worth testing', 'Busy work'];
        const verdict = allowed.find(v => v.toLowerCase() === String(parsed.verdict || '').toLowerCase());
        if (!verdict) return null;

        return { verdict, explanation: parsed.explanation.trim() };
    } catch (err) {
        console.warn('Live idea verdict failed, falling back to the built-in filter:', err.message);
        return null;
    }
}





















